\paragraph{Vision connectors.}
Per-patch MLP projectors~\cite{liu2023llava,kim2024openvla} preserve per-token granularity. Cross-attention connectors (Q-Former~\cite{li2023blip2}, Perceiver~\cite{alayrac2022flamingo,jaegle2021perceiver}) compress to learned queries at the architectural level. Pixel-shuffle connectors~\cite{shi2016subpixel,shukor2025smolvla,chen2024internvl} fold patches via channel concatenation, achieving training-time compression but creating inter-token dependencies. Our analysis links this taxonomy to inference-time compressibility and shows that cross-attention re-encoding can overcome the failure mode of deletion on pixel-shuffle architectures.

\paragraph{VLA token compression.}
Recent concurrent works achieve efficient token compression on MLP-projector VLAs. Compressor-VLA~\cite{kim2026compressorvla} uses instruction-guided cross-attention compression on OpenVLA-OFT. SpecPrune-VLA~\cite{zheng2026specprunevla} applies action-aware self-speculative pruning to OpenVLA with real-robot validation. LightVLA~\cite{wu2026lightvla} introduces differentiable token pruning for VLA efficiency. ``See What Matters''~\cite{lin2026seewhatmatters} proposes grid-based resampling on $\pi_0$ and SmolVLA. SparseVLM~\cite{zhang2025sparsevlm} and SparseVILA~\cite{zhou2025sparsevila} explore token sparsification in VLMs, and ``Don't Run with Scissors''~\cite{li2026scissors} examines weight pruning fragility in VLAs. All compression methods above operate on MLP-projector architectures or evaluate pruning without diagnosing connector-dependent failure modes. Our work is complementary: we identify why deletion-based approaches catastrophically fail on pixel-shuffle architectures, a failure mode not examined by prior work, and validate re-encoding as a remedy.

