\subsection{Design Implications}
\label{sec:design_implications}

Our findings yield three actionable recommendations for VLA deployment:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
\item \textbf{Check before pruning.} Compute the attention entropy ratio on a sample of task-relevant frames before deploying token pruning. If the ratio exceeds 0.9, deletion-based methods are likely to fail; use learned re-encoding instead.
\item \textbf{Connector choice matters for deployment.} If inference-time token reduction is a deployment requirement (e.g., memory-constrained robotic hardware), prefer MLP-projector architectures, which degrade gracefully under pruning.
\item \textbf{Re-encoding recovers compressibility.} For pixel-shuffle VLAs already deployed, inserting a cross-attention re-encoding module (22.2M parameters, 6--7 hours training) can restore token compressibility without retraining the base model.
\end{enumerate}

